# mirrors.manifest — config owned by mod_mirrors (package mirror maintenance).
#
# Layered (update-system.md phase 3):
#   • /usr/share/w/defaults/mirrors.conf   — W's ranking policy. Class managed:
#     overwritten by every update, so a retuned default (the reflector numbers are
#     calibrated, see the file's header) reaches machines that never set their own.
#   • /usr/share/w/defaults/mirrors.schema — who may set each key (all system-scope:
#     there is one mirrorlist, and a user layer over it would let any account steer
#     where the machine downloads its software from).
#   • /etc/w/mirrors.conf — this machine's DEVIATIONS only. Class override: the
#     update never touches it; `w-reset mirrors` drops them back to the vendor layer.
#
# /etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist is deliberately NOT tracked here. It is neither W's file
# nor config: pacman ships it, pacstrap bakes the installer's ranked copy into the
# machine, and w_pac reorders it live during a failing transaction. W rebuilds it
# (`w-mirrors rank`) but never claims ownership of it — declaring it `managed` would
# make an update overwrite a list that is machine state, and `override` would be a
# lie about a file W does not seed.
#
# The timer/service units are static W-owned system files (class managed); the state
# file /var/lib/w/mirrors.state is runtime output, not config.
#
# Format: <path>\t<class>   class: managed | override
/usr/share/w/defaults/mirrors.conf	managed
/usr/share/w/defaults/mirrors.schema	managed
/etc/w/mirrors.conf	override
/usr/lib/systemd/system/w-mirrors.service	managed
/usr/lib/systemd/system/w-mirrors.timer	managed
